.TH HDV 1 "February 2025" "hdv 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hdv \- Hierarchical Data Viewer for CSV files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hdv
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fICSV_FILE\fR]
.PP
.B hdv
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
\fI\- \fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBhdv\fR is an interactive terminal application for exploring CSV data by
drilling down through dimensions. Columns are automatically classified as
numeric (summed when grouped) or non-numeric (dimensions for the hierarchy).
.PP
At the top level, the first dimension column is grouped and numeric columns
are summed per value. Use the arrow keys to select a row and press Right to
expand (drill into) the selected value and see the next dimension. Press Left
to go back one level. A breadcrumb shows the current drill path.
.PP
If a column contains slash-separated paths (e.g. \fI/a/b/c\fR), it can be
expanded into multiple hierarchy levels. Such a column is auto-detected, or
you can specify it with \fB--path-column\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fICOLUMN\fR, \fB--path-column\fR=\fICOLUMN\fR
Treat \fICOLUMN\fR as a slash-separated path and drill by path segments.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fICOLUMN\fR, \fB--string-column\fR=\fICOLUMN\fR
Treat \fICOLUMN\fR as a regular string (disable path auto-detection for it).
May be repeated.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fICOL1,COL2,...\fR, \fB--columns\fR=\fICOL1,COL2,...\fR
Comma-separated list of columns to show (in order). Unlisted columns are hidden.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
Show help message and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fICSV_FILE\fR
Path to a CSV file. If omitted and stdin is not a TTY, reads CSV from stdin.
.TP
\fI\-\fR
Read CSV from stdin (e.g. \fBcat data.csv | hdv -\fR).
.SH KEYBOARD
.TP
\fB\(ua\fR / \fB\(da\fR
Change selected row.
.TP
\fB\(->\fR
Expand (drill into selected value).
.TP
\fB\(<-\fR
Back (one level up).
.TP
\fBs\fR
Toggle sort by dimension label vs numeric column.
.TP
\fBq\fR
Quit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBhdv sales.csv\fR
View \fIsales.csv\fR interactively.
.TP
\fBcat data.csv | hdv\fR
Read CSV from stdin.
.TP
\fBhdv -p path -s label data.csv\fR
Use column \fIpath\fR for hierarchy; treat \fIlabel\fR as a plain string.
.TP
\fBhdv -c name,file,line data.csv\fR
Show only columns \fIname\fR, \fIfile\fR, and \fIline\fR in that order.
